# Lists and tuples

## Lists

### Size or length?

For counting elements in a data structure, Elixir uses `size` if the operation is in constant time (if the value is pre-calculated) or `length` is the operation is linear (of calculating length gets slower as the input grows).

```elixir
list = [1, 2, true, 3]
head = hd(list)
tail = tl(list)
reconstituted = [head] ++ tail
```

#### The mysterious `|` operator:

```elixir
[head | tail] == reconstituted
```

```elixir
[1, 2 | [4]]
```

#### Subtracting items from lists

```elixir
["foo", "bar", "baz"] -- ["bar"]
```

```elixir
["foo", "bar", "foo", "bar"] -- ["bar"]
```

#### Joining lists strings

```elixir
foo_foo = ["foo", "bar", "foo", "bar"] -- ["bar", "bar"]
Enum.join(foo_foo, "")
```

#### Joining strings

```elixir
"foo" <> "bar"
```

#### Splitting a string into a list

```elixir
String.split("foo bar")
```

```elixir
["foo" | String.split("bar baz")] -- ["bar"]
```

#### The | operator

```elixir
# The `|` operator is typically used between brackets to mark the head and tail of a list
[1 | [2, 3, 4]]
```

```elixir
head_tail_ex = [[4, 3, 2] | [1]]
```

```elixir
# Flatten a list
List.flatten(head_tail_ex)
```

### Tuples

```elixir
tuple = {"this", "that"}
```

```elixir
# get a tuple's size
tuple_size(tuple)
```

```elixir
new_tuple = put_elem(tuple, 1, ["is", "a", "test"])
```

```elixir
# Get element at position
elem(new_tuple, 1)
```

```elixir
["this" | elem(new_tuple, 1)]
```
